<section class="section geo-section">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <div class="geo-intro fade-up">
      <div class="section-label">The New Search</div>
      <h2>A Fast Site Is Not Enough if There Is Nothing for AI to&nbsp;Cite.</h2>
      <p class="geo-lead">Your website grade measures whether your site works: speed, mobile, structure. Generative Engine Optimization, or GEO, is a separate question. When a prospect asks an AI assistant for an insurance agency, the answer is built from website content. Agencies with real content get named. Agencies without it do not.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="geo-grid fade-up">
      <article class="geo-card">
        <h3>Clients ask AI before they ask Google</h3>
        <p>Prospects now put their questions to ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Google&rsquo;s AI Overviews. &ldquo;Who is a good independent insurance agency near me.&rdquo; &ldquo;What should I look for in a homeowners policy.&rdquo; The response is a written answer assembled from website content, not a page of blue links to scroll through.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="geo-card">
        <h3>AI engines cite content, not templates</h3>
        <p>Generative engines read and quote substantive pages: coverage explainers, plain-language guides, real answers to the questions clients actually ask. A site that is a homepage, an about page, and a contact form gives an AI nothing to pull from. It does not get cited because there is nothing to cite.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="geo-card">
        <h3>A thin site disappears from the answer</h3>
        <p>When a prospect&rsquo;s AI assistant names three agencies and yours is not one of them, you never know the search happened. No impression, no click, nothing to measure. The agency with content that explains coverage clearly is in the answer. The agency without it is invisible.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="geo-closing fade-up">The grader tells you whether your site is technically healthy. GEO is about whether it has anything worth quoting. <span class="hl">That is a content problem, and content problems are fixable.</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

